2026-08-25T00:00:00Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Zero trust networking
 # uuid: a52dd5d1-9f61-5676-b0b9-02380b784c13
 # tags: rfd:title, rfd:attest-in:none
 RFD:Published  1 RFD
 Serials:Weftspun

2026-08-25T00:00:01Z (RFD66606-1058) '`weftspun_studio/`, `scripts/deploy-weftspun-quadlet.sh`
 # tags: rfd:scope, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:02Z (RFD66606-1058) 'weftspun_studio and its CockroachDB host run as Podman Quadlets on loopback-only ports.
 # tags: rfd:hook
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:03Z (RFD66606-1058) 'A perimeter firewall is not a boundary here. The router and the database run on the same host as every other process the operator runs.
 # tags: rfd:intrigue
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:04Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Run `sudo bash scripts/deploy-weftspun-quadlet.sh`; the sections below hold the verified status.
 # tags: rfd:exit
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:05Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Zero trust networking
 # tags: rfd:details-title, rfd:no-final-newline
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:06Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Run weftspun_studio and its CockroachDB host as Podman Quadlets. A Quadlet is a `.container`, `.volume`, `.network`, or `.build` file. `podman-system-generator` reads it and writes a systemd service.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:07Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Write no hand-written `.service` file. Each unit here is a Quadlet file under `weftspun_studio/deploy/quadlet/`.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:08Z (RFD66606-1058) 'See `DETAILS.md` for the two zero-trust mechanisms, and why CockroachDB replaces the ZooKeeper Manta's metadata tier needed. It also covers the two images, the migrate-before-serve entrypoint, two boot bugs this RFD found and fixed, the deploy command, and the verified status.
 # tags: rfd:decision
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:09Z (RFD66606-1058) 'RFD66606-1019 gives the API server, and RFD66606-1020 pins the CockroachDB build. RFD66606-1037 gives the taskweft job/task split. RFD66606-1055 selects Docker images, on this box first. RFD66606-1057 tracks what is still open. RFD66606-1073 put an S3 server on the Fly toplevel this RFD's loopback rule governed.
 # tags: rfd:related
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:10Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Why the deploy shape
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:11Z (RFD66606-1058) 'RFD66606-1055 selects plain Docker images, on this box first. The router and the CockroachDB host need a deploy shape on this box too, the single 4090 RTX box this project develops on.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:12Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Zero trust, in this deployment
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:13Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Zero trust denies a request by default. It grants access by identity and by context, not by network position. The two mechanisms below give that here.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:14Z (RFD66606-1058) '**A private network per deployment.** `weftspun.network` joins the `weftspun` and `weftspun-crdb` containers. No other container reaches either one by name.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:15Z (RFD66606-1058) '**No port past the loopback address.** Each `PublishPort` binds `127.0.0.1`. A process on this host reaches the API or the database, and a process on the network does not. CockroachDB runs `--insecure` inside `weftspun-crdb`, and that is safe only because of this bind. `deps/cockroach_local`'s own foreground task warns that `--insecure` fits a developer machine. On a single box with loopback-only ports, this container plays that same narrow role.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:16Z (RFD66606-1058) 'SeaweedFS fronts object storage with the S3 API (operator, 2026-11-16). A future worker may read model weights over S3. It would reach SeaweedFS on its own bound port, under the same rule as this section. No port goes past loopback unless a remote caller needs it, and then only that one port.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:17Z (RFD66606-1058) 'RFD66606-1073 once ran `versitygw` on the Fly toplevel, colocated with `weftspun_studio` on `127.0.0.1:10000`. The operator rescinded versitygw on 2026-11-16, and RFD66606-1079 had already removed it. Fly's Tigris and Cloudflare's R2 stay as the hosted S3 stores.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:18Z (RFD66606-1058) 'CockroachDB replaces the Manta metadata tier's ZooKeeper
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:19Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Mark Cavage and David Pacheco's ["Bringing Arbitrary Compute to Authoritative Data"](https://queue.acm.org/detail.cfm?id=2645649) (ACM Queue, 2014) describes Manta, Joyent's object store with in-place compute. Two parts of that design carry over here.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:20Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Manta's metadata tier shards PostgreSQL, fronted by a key/value layer named Moray. A crashed primary needs a new one, chosen by leader election. Manta wrote that election on top of ZooKeeper, because PostgreSQL holds no leader election of its own.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:21Z (RFD66606-1058) 'CockroachDB holds Raft consensus and leader election inside the database. `weftspun-crdb` needs no ZooKeeper, and no Moray, for the same failover Manta built by hand.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:22Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Manta isolates each compute task in its own OS zone, and it rolls that zone back between jobs. A Podman Quadlet container is this project's equivalent. It gives one container per service, its own network namespace, and its own file system. `podman build` tears each one down and rebuilds it from the image. RFD66606-1037's taskweft domains give the job/task split Manta's supervisor and agent gave. The Quadlet gives the isolation Manta's zone gave.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:23Z (RFD66606-1058) 'The images
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:24Z (RFD66606-1058) '`weftspun_studio/Dockerfile` builds the `weftspun_container` release (`mix.exs`). It is a plain `mix release` with no Burrito step. Burrito wraps a release for a host with no Elixir and no Erlang. Inside a container the image is already that unit, so wrapping it again would add a Zig build step for nothing. The bare-metal Burrito release (`weftspun`, same `mix.exs`) still exists for a host with no Podman.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:25Z (RFD66606-1058) '`weftspun_studio/deploy/Dockerfile.crdb` builds CockroachDB from the V-Sekai 22.1 binary `deps/cockroach_local` downloads. It does not use the stock Docker Hub image, so the container matches the version RFD66606-1020 already pins.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:26Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Migrate before serve, inside the container
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:27Z (RFD66606-1058) '`weftspun_studio/deploy/docker-entrypoint.sh` runs `WeftspunStudio.Release.create/0`, then `migrate/0`, then execs the server. No second Quadlet runs the migration as a oneshot step. The entrypoint does it, so the deployment stays to Quadlet-managed containers only.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:28Z (RFD66606-1058) '`Release.create/0` is new. `Ecto.Migrator.with_repo/2` connects to a database. It does not create one. A first boot against an empty CockroachDB node failed with `invalid_catalog_name` before this function existed. `storage_up/1` is idempotent, so the entrypoint calls it every start.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:29Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Two boot bugs this RFD found and fixed
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:30Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Building and running the images surfaced two faults in `WeftspunStudio.Application`. Neither ran before this RFD. RFD66606-1057 marks the Burrito binary "written, and never run." The container path was the first real boot of either fault.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:31Z (RFD66606-1058) '**The halt-after-boot bug.** `application.ex` ran `WeftspunStudio.CLI.main(argv())` and halted whenever `RELEASE_NAME` was set, with no check for empty argv. A standard Elixir release sets `RELEASE_NAME` and passes no argv on `bin/APP start`. A normal release start therefore halted the node right after it booted. The Burrito binary never hit this fault, because Burrito's own launcher sets `__BURRITO_RELEASE_NAME`, not `RELEASE_NAME`, so `release?()` read false there by accident. The fix checks for non-empty argv too, so `db migrate` still reaches the CLI and a bare `start` does not.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:32Z (RFD66606-1058) '**The `Mix.env/0` crash.** `serve?()` called `Mix.env()` to skip the HTTP listener in tests. Mix ships with the compiler, not with a release, so a released node crashed at boot with `UndefinedFunctionError`. The fix guards the call with `Code.ensure_loaded?(Mix)`, the same pattern `argv/0` already used for `Burrito.Util.Args`.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:33Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Deploy
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:34Z (RFD66606-1058) '```bash
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:35Z (RFD66606-1058) 'sudo bash scripts/deploy-weftspun-quadlet.sh
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:36Z (RFD66606-1058) '```
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:37Z (RFD66606-1058) 'The script syncs this repository to `/opt/weftspun/src`, the fixed path the `.build` Quadlets read a `Containerfile` from. It installs the Quadlet files to `/etc/containers/systemd/`, and it starts `weftspun.service`. `weftspun.service` requires `weftspun-crdb.service`, so one command brings up both. The API answers at `http://127.0.0.1:4001`. See Verified, below, for why 4000 is not the port.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:38Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Verified, on this box, through the real Quadlet path
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:39Z (RFD66606-1058) '`scripts/deploy-weftspun-quadlet.sh` ran as root. `podman-system-generator` turned all six Quadlet files into `.service` units. `systemctl status` shows both `weftspun-crdb.service` and `weftspun.service` as `active (running)`, not merely built. `curl http://127.0.0.1:4001/api/v1/health` and `/api/v1/models` both answered, over the Quadlet-managed container, with no Postgrex error in the journal. The app reached CockroachDB by container name (`weftspun-crdb`) over `weftspun.network`.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:40Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Two host-specific faults surfaced only at this last step, past what a direct `podman run` (this RFD's first verification pass) reached.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:41Z (RFD66606-1058) '**Rootful Podman's bridge network could not reach the internet on this host. The kernel dropped it, and DNS was not the cause.** `weftspun-crdb-build.service` failed inside `apt-get update`, timing out on every mirror. DNS was the first suspect (`--dns=1.1.1.1`, still set on both `.build` units). It did not fix the failure. `sudo nft list ruleset` showed why.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:42Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Docker's installer wrote a `FORWARD` chain with `policy drop`. Only two chains it jumps to accept anything: `ts-forward` (Tailscale) and `DOCKER-FORWARD`, which itself drops everything not on `docker0`. Podman's netavark bridge for `weftspun.network` is neither, so the host dropped every packet the build container sent outbound before it left the host. Rootless Podman never hit this chain. It routes through slirp4netns/pasta in user space, not the kernel bridge/forward path rootful Podman uses. That is why the same Dockerfiles built cleanly under rootless Podman earlier in this session.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:43Z (RFD66606-1058) '`DNS=1.1.1.1` on both `.build` units, scoped to the build container only, is the change on record. `sudo nft list ruleset` today shows no accept rule added for the `weftspun` bridge subnet, and the `FORWARD` policy this section describes is still `drop`. A later `deploy-weftspun-quadlet.sh` run built both images and started both services, and `systemctl status` confirms both `weftspun.service` and `weftspun-crdb.service` run today. This RFD records that outcome. It does not claim to explain why the DNS change alone was enough on a retry, when it was not enough on the first attempt.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:44Z (RFD66606-1058) 'A host-wide `nft` `FORWARD` change may still be the more durable fix. Confirm `systemctl status weftspun.service` and `systemctl status weftspun-crdb.service` show `active (running)` after any future rebuild, the same caution RFD66606-1057 gives the dev container and the Pixal3D worker stage.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:45Z (RFD66606-1058) 'The problem
 # tags: rfd:details-heading
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written

2026-08-25T00:00:46Z (RFD66606-1058) 'Trust must come from isolation, not from network position.
 # tags: rfd:details
 Text:RFD1058  1 LINE
 Text:Written
